00:06:15.573 --> 00:06:19.593
So this first question is really for
any of our panelists,

00:06:19.593 --> 00:06:21.653
anyone who wants to chime in.

00:06:21.653 --> 00:06:25.910
But why did you decide to pursue an MBA
and why specifically at Stanford?

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.480
&gt;&gt; So for me, it was very obvious.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:34.520
I'm an engineer's education, and I landed
into consulting for random reasons.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.620
So I always felt like I didn't really
have the background knowledge I needed.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:43.667
So I was learning on the job the whole
time, which was fun and was interesting.

00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:45.735
But I felt like I needed to
sort of build up my skills.

00:06:45.735 --> 00:06:50.125
So an MBA was clear, why Stanford.

00:06:50.125 --> 00:06:53.710
For me, Stanford is very special
in the sense that it allows

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.519
cross disciplinary training and education.

00:06:56.519 --> 00:06:57.850
And for me this was very important.

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:02.751
So as an as an engineer who was working in
sort of business world, I always felt like

00:07:02.751 --> 00:07:07.383
one can add value and innovate when
you're In the middle of two disciplines.

00:07:07.383 --> 00:07:11.192
And I think Stanford does a great job at
allowing that also throughout the MBA

00:07:11.192 --> 00:07:11.736
program.

00:07:11.736 --> 00:07:13.689
Being on campus is one thing and

00:07:13.689 --> 00:07:17.533
being able to take classes across
the street is even better.

00:07:17.533 --> 00:07:23.546
So, for me, the decision was very clear
from the beginning because of that.

00:07:23.546 --> 00:07:28.590
&gt;&gt; Maxim, did you want to talk about why
you decided to do an MBA and why Stanford?

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.874
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think it was a very interesting for

00:07:30.874 --> 00:07:33.602
me because the reason I
decided to join Stanford and

00:07:33.602 --> 00:07:38.350
the reason why I changed my career
afterwards are totally different.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:43.344
So I applied and I got admitted to
Stanford with the idea of becoming

00:07:43.344 --> 00:07:48.110
a partner in private equity and
venture capital fund.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:54.640
And obviously Stanford is the ultimate
university for that I would say.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.469
So actually, I think there were two
schools that were really important for

00:08:00.469 --> 00:08:04.182
such a career, Stanford and
the other school.

00:08:04.182 --> 00:08:10.030
And actually, it was clear choice for
me to go to Stanford on that.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:14.960
Having said that,
over the course of two years,

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:20.260
I actually decided to change
my kind of profession.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:25.091
I spent summer internship working for
a VC company in Silicon Valley and

00:08:25.091 --> 00:08:29.439
decided that it's a great job,
I would love to do it maybe 20,

00:08:29.439 --> 00:08:34.850
30 years after graduation, but
not just after the graduation.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:38.498
So I decided that it's much more fun,
and I think it's much more natural for

00:08:38.498 --> 00:08:42.030
me to manage people rather than
Excel sheets and PowerPoints.

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.780
So I decided that I would go for

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:50.610
any job which takes responsibility and
manage a lot of people.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.954
That's what I need actually
at the end of the day.

00:08:55.933 --> 00:08:59.171
&gt;&gt; The sixth question I'll
direct toward Stefan and Maxim,

00:08:59.171 --> 00:09:03.466
but how have you connected with or
leveraged the alumni community in Europe,

00:09:03.466 --> 00:09:06.785
either personally speaking or
professionally speaking?

00:09:08.864 --> 00:09:12.747
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can speak to that a little bit.

00:09:12.747 --> 00:09:17.366
I decided to move to Germany
with my then pregnant wife,

00:09:17.366 --> 00:09:21.789
American wife,
very unsure how that move would go for

00:09:21.789 --> 00:09:26.920
the two of us, both personally and
professionally.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:32.973
And in the second year, there's a big push
to kind of start connecting with alumni.

00:09:32.973 --> 00:09:37.889
I think a really good starting
point is the alumni database that I

00:09:37.889 --> 00:09:41.270
actually do use quite frequently.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.140
And I just started searching for
people in Germany, for people in Munich

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:49.385
specifically, because we were going to
move to Munich at that point.

00:09:49.385 --> 00:09:53.260
And I remember, and this is a true story,
I know it sounds made up, but

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:54.935
it really is a true story.

00:09:54.935 --> 00:09:59.565
I reached out to a handful of people
in Munich, and then one of them,

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:03.302
well, everybody kind of
emailed back right away, but

00:10:03.302 --> 00:10:08.279
one of them said, hey, that's amazing
that you're moving to Munich.

00:10:08.279 --> 00:10:11.789
Would you be at all interested
in me hosting a dinner so

00:10:11.789 --> 00:10:15.767
that you can get to know a whole
bunch alumni at the same time?

00:10:15.767 --> 00:10:20.437
And I thought that I had no idea where
this was coming from, I didn't know him.

00:10:20.437 --> 00:10:22.837
And he said, yeah, I'd be happy to do it.

00:10:22.837 --> 00:10:26.304
So literally,
after four weeks of being in Germany,

00:10:26.304 --> 00:10:29.010
there was a dinner at somebody's house.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:32.162
I think there were 25 people there or
something, and

00:10:32.162 --> 00:10:34.423
it was an amazing start for me, right,

00:10:34.423 --> 00:10:38.547
not knowing a whole lot of people,
because I had never lived in Munich.

00:10:38.547 --> 00:10:42.374
I grew up in Stuttgart, but
I was gone for so many years.

00:10:42.374 --> 00:10:46.172
And it was an amazing introduction
into kind of like at Stanford.

00:10:46.172 --> 00:10:48.454
It really is like a family in Munich,
right?

00:10:48.454 --> 00:10:52.308
This is not New York or San Francisco,
where there's just hundreds of alumni.

00:10:52.308 --> 00:10:56.282
There's clearly a more
limited amount of them.

00:10:56.282 --> 00:10:58.680
But they were super open.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:00.755
They helped right away.

00:11:00.755 --> 00:11:03.099
I mean,
I've gotten investors through them,

00:11:03.099 --> 00:11:05.159
a bunch of them invested in our business.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:12.060
And I've nothing but
good things to say, right?

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:15.285
Again, it's a smaller network than
you will get from some of the other

00:11:15.285 --> 00:11:18.565
programs in Europe, obviously,
just by the sheer size of the class and

00:11:18.565 --> 00:11:20.440
the geographic location.

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:26.390
But the accessibility and the willingness
to help is very, very high.

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:32.025
&gt;&gt; Maxim, would you like to share your
experiences connecting with alumni?

00:11:32.025 --> 00:11:36.532
&gt;&gt; I think I'll give three
examples of my personal story.

00:11:36.532 --> 00:11:40.820
First is about not professional relevant,
like personal.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:45.573
I totally echo Stefan's
point on the really close

00:11:45.573 --> 00:11:48.969
community in European countries,

00:11:48.969 --> 00:11:54.410
and more family style kind of feeling,
I would say.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:12:00.706
Each of Stanford GSB graduates knows each
other quite well, and we get together with

00:12:00.706 --> 00:12:05.859
the families, and we can go fishing
together, we can go and travel.

00:12:05.859 --> 00:12:09.786
So it really feels like
similar culture and

00:12:09.786 --> 00:12:14.150
similar values,
which are really important,

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:19.056
as well as everyone is so
kind of open to share what he or

00:12:19.056 --> 00:12:25.512
she has with the rest of the community,
GSB community here in Moscow.

00:12:25.512 --> 00:12:30.224
And I also like traveling around
the globe before Coronavirus came about.

00:12:30.224 --> 00:12:35.124
And actually, it's really great
to send an email to any of

00:12:35.124 --> 00:12:40.024
my classmates in any country in the world,
actually, and

00:12:40.024 --> 00:12:43.924
get the best experience
within that country,

00:12:43.924 --> 00:12:48.642
both from a professional and
from personal viewpoint.

00:12:48.642 --> 00:12:52.468
And on the careerwise,

00:12:52.468 --> 00:12:56.876
GSB alumni helped me a lot.

00:12:56.876 --> 00:13:02.141
First, I got my first CEO
position through GSB network,

00:13:02.141 --> 00:13:07.407
and definitely,
it is one of the greatest opportunities

00:13:07.407 --> 00:13:11.265
that alumnis have got after graduation.

00:13:11.265 --> 00:13:16.574
And obviously, it is due to the fact
that we all share the same experience,

00:13:16.574 --> 00:13:20.704
but the idea that actually,
literally, within one day,

00:13:20.704 --> 00:13:25.815
everyone answers your email because
you also graduate from GSB.

00:13:25.815 --> 00:13:29.240
It's what I have experienced a lot.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:34.450
And for example,
my current co-partners, ETSAN,

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:41.180
it's Internet real estate marketplace,
and they are GSBs as well.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:45.994
So they were raised over $1
billion fund from JP Morgan,

00:13:45.994 --> 00:13:49.350
European pension funds, etc.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:54.673
But it was very easier for
us to close the deal, basically,

00:13:54.673 --> 00:14:00.529
to bring investments to ETSAN when
we decided to raise new money,

00:14:00.529 --> 00:14:04.270
because I mean, they think the same way.

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:09.205
They also kind of share the idea
of how to build business together.

00:14:09.205 --> 00:14:13.299
And I think over
the course of seven years,

00:14:13.299 --> 00:14:17.070
I truly feel that we are like-minded.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:20.949
We share the same ideas, and
obviously, we have some arguments, but

00:14:20.949 --> 00:14:24.329
we understand how to resolve
them in a professional manner.

00:14:24.329 --> 00:14:29.136
And that's definitely
another really important

00:14:29.136 --> 00:14:33.260
angle to Stanford community, thank you.

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:35.239
&gt;&gt; Sita,
I'm actually going to cold call you.

00:14:35.239 --> 00:14:39.186
I've heard you talk in the past
about your Stanford GSB tribe.

00:14:39.186 --> 00:14:41.853
Do you want to share
a little bit about that?

00:14:41.853 --> 00:14:43.039
&gt;&gt; Yeah, happy to.

00:14:43.039 --> 00:14:46.714
So this is a post graduation development.

00:14:46.714 --> 00:14:52.522
Actually, Stefan and I are in the same
tribe, if I may share that much.

00:14:52.522 --> 00:14:57.743
So we were a bunch of people who were
going to move away from California,

00:14:57.743 --> 00:15:03.247
and more specifically, to Europe
towards the end of our time at the GSB.

00:15:03.247 --> 00:15:06.802
We knew that we were going to go
directly to Europe, different countries.

00:15:06.802 --> 00:15:10.073
And we really wanted to stay
connected to sort of the school,

00:15:10.073 --> 00:15:14.323
to thinking, that sort of cultural aspect
that Maxim was just talking about.

00:15:14.323 --> 00:15:20.833
And we just started a group of,
initially, I think we had three tribes,

00:15:20.833 --> 00:15:25.741
sort of Latam, US, and
Europe, ten people each, and

00:15:25.741 --> 00:15:29.829
we're still going strong five years later.

00:15:29.829 --> 00:15:34.531
We're basically meeting up about
every quarter, used to be in person,

00:15:34.531 --> 00:15:38.166
now, we've obviously switched
to virtual meetings.

00:15:38.166 --> 00:15:42.624
And it's just a great way to spend
a good chunk of time together.

00:15:42.624 --> 00:15:45.383
We usually do either a full weekend or

00:15:45.383 --> 00:15:50.191
a longer workshop session where
we just kind of share personal or

00:15:50.191 --> 00:15:55.353
professional Issues we're
struggling with are things

00:15:55.353 --> 00:16:00.993
that we want our GSB colleagues input on
and really depending on what people need,

00:16:00.993 --> 00:16:05.346
if they need coaching,
if they need support, whatever it is.

00:16:05.346 --> 00:16:11.828
We try to help each other out and kind of
bring that GSB spirit into our lives and

00:16:11.828 --> 00:16:18.329
it's been such an enriching experience,
I'm very, very thankful for it.

00:16:18.329 --> 00:16:22.977
It takes a lot of, So
you have to really stick with it and

00:16:22.977 --> 00:16:26.157
everybody really needs to get a little
value out of it, because everybody

00:16:26.157 --> 00:16:29.820
obviously starting families and businesses
and has a lot of different priorities.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:35.234
But people have really made it
a priority and that's been great.

00:16:35.234 --> 00:16:37.284
&gt;&gt; Okay, thanks for sharing.

