00:23:28.959 --> 00:23:32.698
And so a lot of the decisions that I
take in my day to day, whether it's

00:23:32.698 --> 00:23:37.359
with restaurant partners, people working
at the labor group, or my own employees.

00:23:37.359 --> 00:23:42.150
It's really trying to go back to
the set of principles that I think

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:46.430
Stanford really helped me tease
out over those two years.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:54.239
And, Stefan, how do you think
the Stanford MBA differentiates you or

00:23:54.239 --> 00:23:56.068
how you approach things?

00:23:56.068 --> 00:23:57.440
&gt;&gt; I think there's two elements.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:00.303
I think there's the kind of like,

00:24:00.303 --> 00:24:05.252
general interest from people
in Europe in Silicon Valley.

00:24:05.252 --> 00:24:08.634
And I think Maxim mentioned earlier
right that Stanford is kind of like at

00:24:08.634 --> 00:24:09.640
the epicenter of it.

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:14.966
So that's a given but that does make
you a, I don't want to say a desirable

00:24:14.966 --> 00:24:19.943
person but I mean I have a lot of
meetings but people are super curious

00:24:19.943 --> 00:24:24.684
about Silicon Valley and
like different approaches to things.

00:24:24.684 --> 00:24:28.484
And it's a very good starting point if
you're trying to connect with somebody

00:24:28.484 --> 00:24:32.342
that you might not otherwise be able to
get in touch with or something like that,

00:24:32.342 --> 00:24:35.566
that kind of like general hi,
let's talk about Silicon Valley and

00:24:35.566 --> 00:24:37.610
what I learned there and what I saw there.

00:24:37.610 --> 00:24:40.707
Is always a really,
really good starting point and

00:24:40.707 --> 00:24:44.321
it's something that people
are genuinely interested in.

00:24:44.321 --> 00:24:49.324
The other thing to me is I think
it's more of a personal thing.

00:24:49.324 --> 00:24:55.944
I was always under the impression before
the NBA that I could learn hard skills.

00:24:55.944 --> 00:24:59.728
Through like many different avenues and

00:24:59.728 --> 00:25:04.857
I really focus on soft skill
development at Stanford.

00:25:04.857 --> 00:25:09.495
I think Stanford itself pushes
you in that direction a lot.

00:25:09.495 --> 00:25:14.367
But that was super important to me and
I think that's a very big differentiating

00:25:14.367 --> 00:25:17.869
factor from what I can tell to
other MBA programs as well.

00:25:17.869 --> 00:25:21.112
And again,
obviously didn't attend those but

00:25:21.112 --> 00:25:25.733
just from talking to a lot of alumni
I certainly have a very different

00:25:25.733 --> 00:25:30.354
approach to kind of like leadership
as Christina mentioned just now

00:25:30.354 --> 00:25:34.920
it's like a very kind of like let's
get in behind a shared vision.

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.939
Let's do it together.

00:25:37.939 --> 00:25:43.570
I think I'm much more aware of how I come
across to others than I was in the past.

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:47.458
I never really thought about it,
because nobody ever told me that I should,

00:25:47.458 --> 00:25:48.630
[LAUGH] quite frankly.

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.988
And there's a lot of like things and

00:25:50.988 --> 00:25:55.858
kind of like also this Silicon Valley
approach of that it's okay to fail,

00:25:55.858 --> 00:26:01.612
that we've definitely implemented in our
company, and we're not worried about it.

00:26:01.612 --> 00:26:06.145
And I think it might be different in
other European countries, but in Germany,

00:26:06.145 --> 00:26:10.220
there's certainly a stigma,
a very negative stigma around failure.

00:26:10.220 --> 00:26:15.992
And we've made it completely okay
obviously under the right circumstances.

00:26:15.992 --> 00:26:20.728
And that's helped us tremendously,
we've been able to achieve

00:26:20.728 --> 00:26:25.304
things that I don't think we
would have with another culture.

