00:27:40.168 --> 00:27:43.222
And I could sort of toss to the topics,

00:27:43.222 --> 00:27:49.246
other GSB classes that really stuck
with me were around HR and leadership.

00:27:49.246 --> 00:27:53.833
And not so much the sort of the ones
that I knew were going to be great that

00:27:53.833 --> 00:27:59.226
everybody talks about, but also the ones
that are a little bit more tangible and

00:27:59.226 --> 00:28:05.051
hands on there was something I don't know
the colloquial term was hiring and firing.

00:28:05.051 --> 00:28:10.445
Really hands on class that
I think about every single

00:28:10.445 --> 00:28:15.358
time I have sort of
managerial topic coming up.

00:28:15.358 --> 00:28:20.338
Acting with power [LAUGH] was a surprising
one that really helped me come out

00:28:20.338 --> 00:28:24.048
of my shell and
then try a different leadership style.

00:28:24.048 --> 00:28:27.358
That I think about a lot.

00:28:27.358 --> 00:28:31.457
And weirdly a statistics
class that I also end up and

00:28:31.457 --> 00:28:36.410
every time somebody comes to me
with some sort of fact or stat or

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:41.364
I read an article in the news,
I question it in a very different

00:28:41.364 --> 00:28:45.163
way than I used to
&gt;&gt; Christina happen you have any favorite

00:28:45.163 --> 00:28:49.231
GSB courses or experiences that
you continue to leverage today?

00:28:49.231 --> 00:28:56.046
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
when I was a GSB I took two sales classes.

00:28:56.046 --> 00:29:01.474
Because if there was one thing that was
felt very uncomfortable was negotiations.

00:29:01.474 --> 00:29:03.356
And I had this almost
like chip on my shoulder.

00:29:03.356 --> 00:29:06.010
So I went I did the took the two classes,
and after that, I was like,

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:07.002
you know this was great.

00:29:07.002 --> 00:29:11.096
I'll never get there into like
a sales role and here yeah,

00:29:11.096 --> 00:29:14.287
I'm like global commercial delivery room.

00:29:14.287 --> 00:29:19.821
And it's funny because,
I go back to those classes every day,

00:29:19.821 --> 00:29:24.103
as in beyond the tools
that you could think well,

00:29:24.103 --> 00:29:29.868
maybe I can get them in there's
plenty of sales classes and so on.

00:29:29.868 --> 00:29:35.601
It was morelike the approach in which
those tools were offered to us and

00:29:35.601 --> 00:29:39.793
so a lot of live sort of like cases and
simulations.

00:29:39.793 --> 00:29:44.190
And then similarly, I took classes
like managing difficult conversations,

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:48.469
managing growing enterprises where again,
it's like you prepare a case.

00:29:48.469 --> 00:29:52.656
You're called out and
you have to basically act pretty much and

00:29:52.656 --> 00:29:54.476
be riding in on the moment.

00:29:54.476 --> 00:29:59.131
And in the role that I have seen
a negotiation table with counterparts,

00:29:59.131 --> 00:30:02.560
lawyers and
it's like everything is in the moment.

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:05.660
I've gotten so much back to it.

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:10.318
I've written back to professors telling
them about something that had happened and

00:30:10.318 --> 00:30:11.968
what was their perspective.

00:30:11.968 --> 00:30:16.903
So definitely helpful probably
in the most unexpected

00:30:16.903 --> 00:30:21.193
way because I didn't think
I was going to get a role

00:30:21.193 --> 00:30:26.144
similar to those classes but
yeah, it's been great.

00:30:26.144 --> 00:30:30.274
&gt;&gt; This next question I'll have Maxime
