00:32:20.850 --> 00:32:25.929
And if you have the Russian
guys who studied at Harvard,

00:32:25.929 --> 00:32:31.781
Wharton and Chicago at that time,
who at Stanford University.

00:32:31.781 --> 00:32:36.966
Who were like one-two years
prior to my graduation.

00:32:36.966 --> 00:32:42.411
They also became friends of mine since
that week, so there was one piece

00:32:42.411 --> 00:32:48.336
of advice just network a lot within
this community prayer enduring justice.

00:32:48.336 --> 00:32:53.003
And the second one, I personally believe
that Stanford has great resources.

00:32:53.003 --> 00:32:57.709
Although, I truly at this 15 years ago,

00:32:57.709 --> 00:33:05.963
it was the case that I relied more on
myself rather than on official venues.

00:33:05.963 --> 00:33:10.085
If I would love to work back
in the home country in Russia.

00:33:10.085 --> 00:33:14.482
And I definitely decided
that I graduated first, and

00:33:14.482 --> 00:33:18.000
then come back, and then start looking for

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:23.289
a job rather than trying to do
both like partying during the day.

00:33:23.289 --> 00:33:28.050
Working during the night,
trying to find the job you're in like

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:32.471
summer/winter trying to find
a job during morning hours.

00:33:32.471 --> 00:33:33.496
It doesn't work out.

00:33:33.496 --> 00:33:38.504
So I prefer just to focus more
on understanding the classes and

00:33:38.504 --> 00:33:44.588
learning some something talking and
parting with my friends during GSBs.

00:33:44.588 --> 00:33:47.528
And then I graduated, come back and

00:33:47.528 --> 00:33:52.431
then I started actually looking for
a new job like.

00:33:52.431 --> 00:33:54.087
Anyone else have advice, Christina, yeah?

00:33:54.087 --> 00:33:55.693
Yeah, I can jump in.

00:33:55.693 --> 00:34:02.684
So I actually did look into
moving to Europe right after GSB.

00:34:02.684 --> 00:34:06.938
And so I took through the summer
in between the two years

00:34:06.938 --> 00:34:10.464
the opportunity to come to London for
a week.

00:34:10.464 --> 00:34:14.665
Obviously, I've done sort of like a bit
of prep work up front a bit maybe

00:34:14.665 --> 00:34:15.797
what industries.

00:34:15.797 --> 00:34:17.450
And it was very bad way,

00:34:17.450 --> 00:34:22.243
it's not that I had very clear it has
to be consumer or attack like I was

00:34:22.243 --> 00:34:26.472
very sort of wide spectrum of
things that I saw myself doing.

00:34:26.472 --> 00:34:31.869
because also that's what you do an MBA for
sort of and they're sending that up.

00:34:31.869 --> 00:34:35.259
So my advice would be start early and

00:34:35.259 --> 00:34:39.899
do not [INAUDIBLE] the schedule
is busy at the GSB.

00:34:39.899 --> 00:34:45.194
But there's always that one week and
you don't even have to be in the place,

00:34:45.194 --> 00:34:47.855
but just allocate time early enough.

00:34:47.855 --> 00:34:52.157
And I did keep in touch with the alumni
that I had pinged during that summer,

00:34:52.157 --> 00:34:55.777
because I think one thing to remember
is that, alumni are people and

00:34:55.777 --> 00:34:59.084
they're very nice people and
they like to stay in touch.

00:34:59.084 --> 00:35:02.346
You're not just simply asking them for
a job, you're making a human connection.

00:35:02.346 --> 00:35:06.106
So I found that a lot of alumni
have stayed in touch with.

00:35:06.106 --> 00:35:11.085
And then I actually quote and
quote, use them leverage them as

00:35:11.085 --> 00:35:15.795
I was deciding what I wanted
to go back immediately or not.

00:35:15.795 --> 00:35:20.348
In sort of understanding,
what was best for me and so

00:35:20.348 --> 00:35:25.947
I ended up taking a job in the US,
I didn't move straight after.

00:35:25.947 --> 00:35:30.854
But when I then was back in London,
it took me two weeks to find a new job and

00:35:30.854 --> 00:35:33.363
I had like plenty of opportunities.

00:35:33.363 --> 00:35:37.917
So it was very much, yeah,
I guess, I would say start early.

00:35:37.917 --> 00:35:41.004
You don't have to go to them and
say you're 100% sure,

00:35:41.004 --> 00:35:43.548
you're going to move to Europe,
be transparent.

00:35:43.548 --> 00:35:47.499
People are not expecting you to take
any decision during your first year and

00:35:47.499 --> 00:35:49.328
so that gives you a lot of freedom.

00:35:49.328 --> 00:35:55.399
I think I'm in shaping a bit
your career after GSB.

00:35:55.399 --> 00:35:58.575
Great, thank you.

00:35:58.575 --> 00:36:01.835
Well, many of your responses have
addressed some of the questions that we

00:36:01.835 --> 00:36:02.948
have from our audience.

00:36:02.948 --> 00:36:07.396
But I want to specifically dive into
some of those now that are open for

00:36:07.396 --> 00:36:08.628
anyone to answer.

00:36:08.628 --> 00:36:11.666
We have a question here
of it's sort of twofold.

00:36:11.666 --> 00:36:17.077
What was the most unexpected or impactful
experience during your time at Stanford,

00:36:17.077 --> 00:36:20.978
and maybe what was one of
the more challenging experiences?

00:36:20.978 --> 00:36:23.779
So who would like to tackle one of those?

00:36:27.551 --> 00:36:30.887
I can say in terms of unexpected.

00:36:30.887 --> 00:36:35.562
If somebody then wants to take the next
one, there's touchy feeling as a class,

00:36:35.562 --> 00:36:38.752
which everybody in which
Stanford is very famous for.

00:36:38.752 --> 00:36:40.943
And there are a lot of people
who go to Stanford and

00:36:40.943 --> 00:36:43.846
who are just 100% sure they
will participate in that class.

00:36:43.846 --> 00:36:46.925
And that's one of the big events
of their Stanford program.

00:36:46.925 --> 00:36:48.614
I was not on that line of thinking,

00:36:48.614 --> 00:36:52.242
I was more on the line of thinking work
with everybody does it, I will not.

00:36:52.242 --> 00:36:55.130
So I didn't take it
until the end actually,

00:36:55.130 --> 00:36:59.500
when I had a little bit of fear of
missing out in the last semester and

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:04.589
I registered just in case, there was
something important happening there.

00:37:04.589 --> 00:37:08.296
And although I resisted through the first
classes where I was still trying to get

00:37:08.296 --> 00:37:09.577
convinced about the idea.

00:37:09.577 --> 00:37:12.550
I have to say for me,
and very surprisingly,

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:17.168
it was one of the best experiences
I've done, not only at Stanford but

00:37:17.168 --> 00:37:19.455
also I would say beyond Stanford.

00:37:19.455 --> 00:37:23.682
The level of human connection.

00:37:23.682 --> 00:37:27.784
So it's not really about content,
it's really about relationship.

00:37:27.784 --> 00:37:33.172
The level of human connection that
we experienced during that class and

00:37:33.172 --> 00:37:38.846
the conversations we could or we manage
to have on a person to person basis.

00:37:38.846 --> 00:37:42.399
Tying to understand what Stephen
was saying how you come across and

00:37:42.399 --> 00:37:45.951
how others view you, which you may
not have considered until now,

00:37:45.951 --> 00:37:48.442
because you'll never
just thought about it.

00:37:48.442 --> 00:37:53.530
This I found the most I have to say after
the two years the most powerful and

00:37:53.530 --> 00:37:59.220
unexpected event for me, at least from my
the many classes that I took at the GSB.

00:38:01.995 --> 00:38:04.257
The other unexpected surprises or

00:38:04.257 --> 00:38:07.539
impactful experiences
folks want to highlight?

00:38:07.539 --> 00:38:12.425
I think I've got three unexpected
surprises that I would

00:38:12.425 --> 00:38:14.675
probably share with you.

00:38:14.675 --> 00:38:22.871
First, I didn't expect, such a beautiful
nature around the Stanford University and

00:38:22.871 --> 00:38:27.360
just going out in the woods,
and data lake, and

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:31.975
the mountains and
ocean in the close proximity.

00:38:31.975 --> 00:38:36.527
With that definitely made difference for
me and for my family.

00:38:36.527 --> 00:38:41.926
And the second one, I will say that
I came with the son of mine too and

00:38:41.926 --> 00:38:47.137
my spouse to the campus, and
I didn't expect kindergartens and

00:38:47.137 --> 00:38:50.252
schools of such high quality of care.

00:38:50.252 --> 00:38:55.402
I definitely recommend for
those who would come with families

00:38:55.402 --> 00:39:00.064
to consider that option on
the Stanford campus person.

00:39:00.064 --> 00:39:04.584
And there are so many things happening and

00:39:04.584 --> 00:39:12.463
we were told when they came on the first
day that it is really important for

00:39:12.463 --> 00:39:18.809
us to build this skill how to
manage our schedule, right?

00:39:18.809 --> 00:39:23.285
Both including sleeping and
network, and so many clubs, so

00:39:23.285 --> 00:39:27.339
many classes, so
many great people you want to talk to.

00:39:27.339 --> 00:39:31.901
It's like drinking from a firehose,
I would say, and

00:39:31.901 --> 00:39:38.559
it's definitely one of the first abilities
I had to manage in order to survive.

00:39:38.559 --> 00:39:43.593
Basically, I didn't expect that, but
it helped a lot after graduation as well.

00:39:43.593 --> 00:39:48.636
[LAUGH] I can quickly jump in.

00:39:48.636 --> 00:39:53.940
Before the GSB,
my life was pretty straight lined, right,

00:39:53.940 --> 00:40:00.083
I did like economics undergrad,
I had a company and things like this.

00:40:00.083 --> 00:40:05.856
And so in terms of unexpected
impactful things, I was very hesitant

00:40:05.856 --> 00:40:12.050
to take kind of strange classes that
didn't just check off my boxes off.

00:40:12.050 --> 00:40:14.998
This has a one on one correlation
to make me a better or

00:40:14.998 --> 00:40:17.820
whatever manager leader or
something like this.

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:24.137
The best class in my opinion was
a mindfulness class that I took.

00:40:24.137 --> 00:40:27.389
I don't even know what department,
it wasn't the GSB,

00:40:27.389 --> 00:40:31.543
I went in there on the advice of my
wife who said you should really do this.

00:40:31.543 --> 00:40:35.414
And I said, okay, when will I
ever do this other than now and

00:40:35.414 --> 00:40:39.681
Sita knows me well,
that's what she's laughing and I loved it.

00:40:39.681 --> 00:40:41.385
And if there's one piece of advice and

00:40:41.385 --> 00:40:43.846
I don't know if this is my
place to give you advice now.

00:40:43.846 --> 00:40:48.942
But I would drop your expectations and
not just focus on the things

00:40:48.942 --> 00:40:54.781
that you think have a one on one
correlation to making you more successful,

00:40:54.781 --> 00:40:58.595
whatever your goal is richer and
more powerful.

00:40:58.595 --> 00:41:03.875
Take those two years and literally
pump the brakes for just one second,

00:41:03.875 --> 00:41:08.890
read the whatever 100 electives,
especially in the second year

00:41:08.890 --> 00:41:14.271
that you can take, and just take some
crazy ones that just interest you.

00:41:14.271 --> 00:41:16.231
And what's the worst thing
that can happen to you?

00:41:16.231 --> 00:41:19.947
You go to two classes, and
then you just like don't go for

00:41:19.947 --> 00:41:24.608
the rest of the semester, and I wish
I would have done a lot more of that.

00:41:24.608 --> 00:41:28.567
And in terms of the challenge,
that is kind of also the challenge, right?

00:41:28.567 --> 00:41:30.167
Because once you start doing that,

00:41:30.167 --> 00:41:33.377
there aren't going to be two classes
that you're kind of interested in.

00:41:33.377 --> 00:41:37.292
There's going to be 25 and
it's 25 a semester.

00:41:37.292 --> 00:41:41.163
So you have to just be okay
with saying look, I'd love to,

00:41:41.163 --> 00:41:43.773
I'm kind of interested in these 25.

00:41:43.773 --> 00:41:48.343
But I can only do two because otherwise,
my schedule is so

00:41:48.343 --> 00:41:52.735
crazy that I'm missing out
on a million other things.

00:41:52.735 --> 00:41:56.668
&gt;&gt; Any other challenging experiences
during your time at Stanford?

00:42:00.578 --> 00:42:05.690
&gt;&gt; I think the challenges
are always not being dragged by

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:11.822
what a lot of people do or
I think what we were just talking about,

00:42:11.822 --> 00:42:18.313
sort of what you think is going to be
good for you, and for your career.

00:42:18.313 --> 00:42:23.825
And sort of staying grounded
while you're trying to achieve,

00:42:23.825 --> 00:42:28.931
and that also becomes sometimes
very confused and foggy.

00:42:28.931 --> 00:42:34.709
And so at the time I felt that I
was almost sort of getting lost and

00:42:34.709 --> 00:42:37.559
not getting the most out of it.

00:42:37.559 --> 00:42:39.370
And then now, a couple of years out,

00:42:39.370 --> 00:42:43.530
I actually realized that that's actually
the process you're meant to go through.

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:47.080
You don't go to the GSB
because you have it all clear.

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:51.814
If anything is an opportunity to
admit that not everything is clear,

00:42:51.814 --> 00:42:55.090
also life doesn't go
always as you planned it.

00:42:55.090 --> 00:43:00.835
So there's that element and that becomes
even more true as you graduate and

00:43:00.835 --> 00:43:04.130
life moves on for
a lot of your classmates.

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:08.624
And so I think the challenge in the moment
is always sort of being mindful of

00:43:08.624 --> 00:43:11.233
that and
not getting scared of not starting

00:43:11.233 --> 00:43:15.383
doubting yourself that maybe you're
not doing enough or too much.

00:43:15.383 --> 00:43:20.136
They always say you can either sleep and
not go to the party, or

00:43:20.136 --> 00:43:25.090
finish your assignments, and
get a good grade, or go to class.

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:28.889
It's just there's too much to do
that sometimes it gets overwhelming.

00:43:31.107 --> 00:43:35.002
&gt;&gt; This next question,
maybe Sita, we'll have you start.

00:43:35.002 --> 00:43:38.466
And Stefan, you might have a perspective
here having spent, I think

00:43:38.466 --> 00:43:42.542
you said 17 years in the US, but we're
seeing some questions from panelists.

00:43:42.542 --> 00:43:47.718
If you are not originally a European
citizen or if you're coming from

00:43:47.718 --> 00:43:53.535
another part of the world and want to
transition to Europe, is that possible?

00:43:53.535 --> 00:43:58.107
And maybe you can even reflect all of you
on your peers, and your colleagues, and

00:43:58.107 --> 00:44:00.944
your classmates in your
various home countries.

00:44:00.944 --> 00:44:05.409
Do you see non-European
citizens who are now currently

00:44:05.409 --> 00:44:08.362
working in Europe who are GSB grads?

00:44:08.362 --> 00:44:12.267
And Sita I called on you because
of your personal connection there.

00:44:12.267 --> 00:44:15.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] yeah, I'm happy to share.

00:44:15.025 --> 00:44:17.462
So this was my unexpected.

00:44:17.462 --> 00:44:21.568
I didn't want to put it in
the challenge or unexpected thing, but

00:44:21.568 --> 00:44:24.548
I met my husband at
the GSB who's an American.

00:44:24.548 --> 00:44:29.246
I thought I wanted to come
back to Europe after the GSB,

00:44:29.246 --> 00:44:34.365
met this American guy unexpected,
not what I had planned.

00:44:34.365 --> 00:44:42.452
Yeah, the way he did it was he had
worked in the summer internship.

00:44:42.452 --> 00:44:48.404
He worked for
a then USS startup in product and

00:44:48.404 --> 00:44:55.468
they were just in the process
of expanding to Europe.

00:44:55.468 --> 00:45:00.518
And he basically got an offer from them
for the Silicon Valley office, but

00:45:00.518 --> 00:45:05.667
then managed to transfer that over to
the UK, so they were sponsoring him.

00:45:05.667 --> 00:45:10.269
So that's one of the paths
that I know pretty well.

00:45:10.269 --> 00:45:13.100
Obviously, we've since gotten married, so

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:18.192
that's another thing that is sometimes
possible and sometimes, obviously not.

00:45:18.192 --> 00:45:20.072
The other thing that he also did, so

00:45:20.072 --> 00:45:24.097
I mentioned that I had an offer from
a consulting firm that I had worked with.

00:45:24.097 --> 00:45:26.951
And so we tried Amsterdam over
the summer by the way and

00:45:26.951 --> 00:45:29.182
then decided we wanted to move to London.

00:45:29.182 --> 00:45:32.505
So that was another little thing there.

00:45:32.505 --> 00:45:37.657
Obviously try whatever you can, but
consulting obviously has global

00:45:37.657 --> 00:45:42.648
recruiting and they obviously know
how to do all of the visa things.

00:45:42.648 --> 00:45:47.022
So that's a fairly safe one I would say.

00:45:47.022 --> 00:45:51.186
You can even do an internship in one city,
get an offer,

00:45:51.186 --> 00:45:56.784
transfer that offer or apply directly
with the office that you want to go to.

00:45:56.784 --> 00:45:59.556
So that's how I went to Amsterdam and

00:45:59.556 --> 00:46:03.770
we would have been able to go
to London with that as well.

00:46:03.770 --> 00:46:08.692
Other friends, yeah,
unfortunately I'm aware of a few of

00:46:08.692 --> 00:46:12.926
our classmates who stayed in the US,
but didn't,

00:46:12.926 --> 00:46:18.650
in the former presidents era,
didn't manage to quite stay longer.

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:22.621
Some of them have come to Europe, I don't
know exactly the visa situation, but

00:46:22.621 --> 00:46:26.180
it seemed to be significantly easier
obviously than staying in the US at

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:26.738
the time.

00:46:26.738 --> 00:46:31.986
I think hopefully that'll
loosen up [LAUGH] my future,

00:46:31.986 --> 00:46:36.672
but those are the things
I can think of right now.

00:46:36.672 --> 00:46:41.294
&gt;&gt; And to just add like one or
two more things.

00:46:41.294 --> 00:46:42.997
I mean, apart from immigration things,

00:46:42.997 --> 00:46:45.733
right, which I don't know anything
about how difficult that is.

00:46:45.733 --> 00:46:49.464
But I think like Sita said,
for sure big companies, right,

00:46:49.464 --> 00:46:51.598
are always a good starting point.

00:46:51.598 --> 00:46:56.250
And I can just talk for
little Munich as my wife calls it.

00:46:56.250 --> 00:46:59.582
She's from New York, so
it's very little to her.

00:46:59.582 --> 00:47:04.491
But even there, right, there's plenty of
English speaking opportunities as well.

00:47:04.491 --> 00:47:10.304
So that's clearly not an issue in London
or any of the other bigger cities, but so

00:47:10.304 --> 00:47:15.968
even the language thing, I think because
that's a big one for a lot of people.

00:47:15.968 --> 00:47:21.000
You can do without, I mean, my wife did
not speak a word of German when we moved

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:25.968
and she's now speak some, but I don't
think that should be a huge concern.

00:47:25.968 --> 00:47:29.634
But I would come back to the alumni,
right, as the first starting point.

00:47:29.634 --> 00:47:34.783
I mean, there's plenty of
alumni in whatever important

00:47:34.783 --> 00:47:39.513
powerful positions in
virtually every big cities.

00:47:39.513 --> 00:47:43.873
So if there's a larger city that you're
interested in or a company you're

00:47:43.873 --> 00:47:48.731
interested in, right, with somebody from
another office, I would start there.

00:47:48.731 --> 00:47:54.014
And I'm sure if you If you're serious
about wanting to move to Europe, there's

00:47:54.014 --> 00:47:58.257
opportunities that the Stanford network
can help identify together with you.

00:47:58.257 --> 00:48:01.480
And you can then make the move.

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:04.882
I mean, we have several just coming
back to this tribe that Sita was talking

00:48:04.882 --> 00:48:07.639
about earlier, our little ten
person group here in Europe.

00:48:07.639 --> 00:48:12.700
There are several Americans on
it that moved to Europe and

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:16.293
are now still here and part of our group.

00:48:16.293 --> 00:48:20.812
So it's definitely a longer term
alternative also for people.

00:48:22.202 --> 00:48:26.860
Okay, we have a question here
from an audience member.

00:48:26.860 --> 00:48:31.380
Obviously, there are a number of wonderful
European business schools as well.

00:48:31.380 --> 00:48:36.349
I don't know if any of you are considering
those, but what are the things that you

00:48:36.349 --> 00:48:41.264
felt you could only learn doing your MBA
in the US or maybe the GSB in particular?

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:46.569
[CROSSTALK] I'm sorry, go ahead.

00:48:46.569 --> 00:48:47.447
Go, go, go.

00:48:47.447 --> 00:48:50.400
Okay, I'll quickly do mine.

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:51.782
This was the reason for

00:48:51.782 --> 00:48:56.260
me to decide to go to Stanford because
I visited a couple different schools.

00:48:56.260 --> 00:48:59.834
And the one thing for
me is that at Stanford,

00:48:59.834 --> 00:49:05.340
you have the protagonist of the case
sitting there in front of you and

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:09.989
telling you the story and
all of the back story behind it.

00:49:09.989 --> 00:49:12.547
I can read a case at home,
I can think about what I would do, and

00:49:12.547 --> 00:49:14.581
I could maybe discuss it
with some smart people.

00:49:14.581 --> 00:49:19.161
But hearing from the founder or something
about the struggle they were facing and

00:49:19.161 --> 00:49:21.381
what really went on behind the scenes,

00:49:21.381 --> 00:49:25.810
the things that they maybe didn't
necessarily put in the case.

00:49:25.810 --> 00:49:30.190
That's the kind of learning where I
thought I can only get that here.

00:49:30.190 --> 00:49:34.039
This is Silicon Valley,
a lot of the alums are still around.

00:49:34.039 --> 00:49:35.390
That just blew my mind.

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:41.910
I think [INAUDIBLE] other than that,
sorry, [INAUDIBLE] but

00:49:41.910 --> 00:49:45.069
like I took a class in which they invited.

00:49:45.069 --> 00:49:50.864
So there were two cofounders that
started a company then they separated,

00:49:50.864 --> 00:49:53.022
not in sort of great terms.

00:49:53.022 --> 00:49:55.991
And the professor invites both
of them in two different days.

00:49:55.991 --> 00:49:58.320
And you hear both [INAUDIBLE],

00:49:58.320 --> 00:50:03.550
which it almost gets uncomfortable for
the class we hear, right?

00:50:03.550 --> 00:50:07.369
because it's like you hear one and
then you feel you're on their side, and

00:50:07.369 --> 00:50:09.850
then you go and
you hear the other one of them.

00:50:09.850 --> 00:50:11.950
It's just like I think I agree.

00:50:11.950 --> 00:50:14.420
I mean, it's one of the elements
that made me pick Stanford.

00:50:15.500 --> 00:50:22.261
The other one for me was I wanted to get
a bit of the Silicon Valley experience,

00:50:22.261 --> 00:50:27.890
which in reality for
me meant do something that I'd never done.

00:50:27.890 --> 00:50:30.909
I started a shoe company
while at Stanford.

00:50:30.909 --> 00:50:34.039
And my family's absolutely
not entrepreneurial,

00:50:34.039 --> 00:50:36.891
so for me it was going way
out of my comfort zone.

00:50:36.891 --> 00:50:40.261
And then in Silicon Valley there were
all these people so excited about me and

00:50:40.261 --> 00:50:40.900
my business.

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:44.135
But yeah,
the case was definitely one of the big,

00:50:44.135 --> 00:50:47.070
big things for
me that made me pick Stanford.

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:52.398
Patricia.

00:50:52.398 --> 00:50:56.001
So the MBA is either one year or two year
program, so it's quite the long time.

00:50:56.001 --> 00:50:58.730
And I felt it was a long time
to not work actually, and

00:50:58.730 --> 00:51:00.480
not make a professional career.

00:51:00.480 --> 00:51:03.090
So I wanted to make the best out of it.

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:05.884
And for me, going to the west,
especially going to Stanford and

00:51:05.884 --> 00:51:08.270
Silicon Valley also meant
the cultural experience.

00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:12.412
So it's one thing to get the class and to
understand your statistical concepts and

00:51:12.412 --> 00:51:14.180
even the case and discuss it.

00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:18.602
But it's another thing to experience
how life is in Silicon Valley,

00:51:18.602 --> 00:51:21.130
how people think, how people behave.

00:51:21.130 --> 00:51:23.923
I mean, there's a lot of different
elements that are subjective

00:51:23.923 --> 00:51:26.480
that you don't really measure,
are not written on paper.

00:51:27.500 --> 00:51:30.509
I would not have gotten it if I
would not have gone to Stanford.

00:51:30.509 --> 00:51:34.777
And for me, that was the one of the key
elements in taking the decision to really

00:51:34.777 --> 00:51:36.920
have this comprehensive experience.

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:40.040
So not only classes,
but going beyond that.

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:43.550
Great, thank you.

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:48.490
Well, I do want to be mindful of
the time as I know we're coming up on

00:51:48.490 --> 00:51:50.480
our end point here.

00:51:50.480 --> 00:51:54.028
Again, you'll see another
poll pop up momentarily and

00:51:54.028 --> 00:51:56.870
we'd love to get your input there.

00:51:56.870 --> 00:52:01.222
I want to absolutely thank all of our
panelists for sharing your experiences

00:52:01.222 --> 00:52:06.210
today, both about your time at the GSB, as
well as your time now currently in Europe.

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:09.323
I think it was very, very helpful
to hear about that transition and

00:52:09.323 --> 00:52:11.390
how you made that transition.

00:52:11.390 --> 00:52:13.233
For all of our attendees today,

00:52:13.233 --> 00:52:17.140
we hope that this is just the start
of your Stanford MBA journey.

00:52:17.140 --> 00:52:22.125
And a wonderful way to continue that
journey is through a site called

00:52:22.125 --> 00:52:23.620
MyStanford.mba.

00:52:23.620 --> 00:52:25.730
Please check this out
if you haven't already.

00:52:25.730 --> 00:52:29.409
There's an opportunity to create your
own personalized dashboard there.

00:52:29.409 --> 00:52:34.416
It will tee up clubs and
courses that might be of interest to you,

00:52:34.416 --> 00:52:39.240
student or alumni profiles
that might resonate with you.

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:42.715
If you start an application you can
track your application progress there.

00:52:42.715 --> 00:52:46.695
And it'll also suggest other events
that might be of interest to you,

00:52:46.695 --> 00:52:47.985
such as the one today.

00:52:47.985 --> 00:52:50.045
So again, thank you to our panelists.

00:52:50.045 --> 00:52:51.705
Thank you to all of our attendees.

00:52:51.705 --> 00:52:53.655
I hope you've enjoyed today's session.

00:52:53.655 --> 00:52:55.916
And best of luck in your MBA journey.

